cli (wasm): an allocator that refreshes the thread's view of shared memory (homescoop#1127)

V8 bounds-checks bulk-memory memory.fill/memory.copy (memset, memcpy) on a
thread against that thread's cached memory length, which can lag a
memory.grow another thread's sbrk just did. malloc then hands this thread
a block above its view, and the first memset/memcpy into it traps
"memory access out of bounds" (plain stores work; azw9's analysis in the
slicc-kernel lane: ~12% of rounds in a Node repro, 13 of 120 pnpm agent
installs on CI). pnpm's global allocator on wasm wraps the system one:
a block that ends above memory.size gets a memory.grow(0), which refreshes
the view, before it is returned; realloc is alloc + refresh + copy + free,
so dlmalloc's internal memcpy never meets a stale length.

Upstream: not filed (Lars decides).
removeWhen: V8 updates other threads' memory length before a grown
block can reach them, or wasi-libc's malloc refreshes it.

--- a/pnpm/crates/cli/src/bin/main.rs
+++ b/pnpm/crates/cli/src/bin/main.rs
@@ -1,3 +1,80 @@
+// homescoop#1127: on wasm, pnpm's allocator refreshes this thread's view of
+// the shared memory before handing out memory above it (see WasmAlloc).
+#[cfg(target_family = "wasm")]
+#[global_allocator]
+static ALLOC: WasmAlloc = WasmAlloc;
+
+/// The system allocator (wasi-libc's dlmalloc), plus a refresh of V8's
+/// view of the shared memory (homescoop#1127).
+///
+/// When another thread's `sbrk` has just grown the memory (`memory.grow`)
+/// and malloc hands this thread a block from the new pages, this thread's
+/// `memory.size` can still report the old length until V8 next updates it,
+/// at an interrupt or a stack check. Plain stores to the block work, but
+/// bulk-memory `memory.fill`/`memory.copy` (what memset and memcpy compile
+/// to) are bounds-checked against the stale length and trap "memory access
+/// out of bounds". That was the intermittent trap on rayon workers in warm
+/// installs (azw9's analysis, slicc-kernel lane). `memory.grow(0)` on this
+/// thread refreshes the view, so a block that ends above it gets one before
+/// it's returned. `realloc` is done as alloc + refresh + copy + free, so
+/// dlmalloc's own memcpy never runs into a block this thread can't see yet.
+/// A block below the view (the common case) costs one `memory.size`.
+#[cfg(target_family = "wasm")]
+struct WasmAlloc;
+
+#[cfg(target_family = "wasm")]
+impl WasmAlloc {
+    #[inline]
+    fn seen(ptr: *mut u8, size: usize) {
+        use core::arch::wasm32::{memory_grow, memory_size};
+        if !ptr.is_null() && (ptr as usize).saturating_add(size) > memory_size::<0>() * 65536 {
+            memory_grow::<0>(0);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(target_family = "wasm")]
+unsafe impl std::alloc::GlobalAlloc for WasmAlloc {
+    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: std::alloc::Layout) -> *mut u8 {
+        // SAFETY: forwarded to the system allocator with the same layout.
+        let ptr = unsafe { std::alloc::System.alloc(layout) };
+        Self::seen(ptr, layout.size());
+        ptr
+    }
+
+    unsafe fn alloc_zeroed(&self, layout: std::alloc::Layout) -> *mut u8 {
+        // calloc zeroes with memset inside libc, so allocate, refresh, then zero.
+        // SAFETY: as alloc; the block is `layout.size()` bytes when non-null.
+        unsafe {
+            let ptr = self.alloc(layout);
+            if !ptr.is_null() {
+                std::ptr::write_bytes(ptr, 0, layout.size());
+            }
+            ptr
+        }
+    }
+
+    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: std::alloc::Layout) {
+        // SAFETY: ptr came from System through this allocator with layout.
+        unsafe { std::alloc::System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }
+    }
+
+    unsafe fn realloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: std::alloc::Layout, new_size: usize) -> *mut u8 {
+        // SAFETY: the caller guarantees ptr/layout describe a live block and
+        // new_size is valid for layout's alignment; the new block holds at
+        // least min(old, new) bytes, copied before the old one is freed.
+        unsafe {
+            let new_layout = std::alloc::Layout::from_size_align_unchecked(new_size, layout.align());
+            let new = self.alloc(new_layout);
+            if !new.is_null() {
+                std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(ptr, new, layout.size().min(new_size));
+                self.dealloc(ptr, layout);
+            }
+            new
+        }
+    }
+}
+
 fn main() -> std::process::ExitCode {
     pnpm_cli::main()
 }
